WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
On Thursday, a projectile struck Saudi Aramco's Jazan refinery in southwestern Saudi Arabia,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.120
killing a Nepalese worker and injuring several others.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.480
Saudi Arabia's energy ministry said the strike started a fire, later brought under control,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:20.400
and temporarily halted some operations at the refinery, which processes up to 400,000 barrels

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.680
of crude a day. The ministry did not name the attacker, but the Saudi-led coalition blamed

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.880
Yemen's Iran-aligned Houthis and said it destroyed a missile launch platform in the Yemeni port of

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:37.120
Hodeidah used in the attack. The strike came a day after the Houthis claimed attacks on Ras Tanura,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.680
the kingdom's largest refinery, and the Red Sea ports of Jeddah and Jazan,

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.120
fighting between the two sides reignited in July after a ceasefire collapsed.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:53.200
The attacks are moving oil prices. Brent crude reached $97.30 on Friday,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.200
It's highest since November 9th.

